<Base
  title="Founders Edition"
  description="A twelve-week outline: the Greek and Roman sources of the American republic, read in the same weekly pattern as the main course."
>
  <header class="page-head wrap-narrow">
    <p class="kicker">Twelve weeks · In outline</p>
    <h1>Founders Edition</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Read what the American founders read: the Greek and Roman sources of the
      republic, in their own words and languages, and walk the buildings they
      raised to them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow prose">
      <h2>A first door, or a bridge</h2>
      <p>
        It is for the same reader as the main course, especially Americans curious
        about the classical roots of the Constitution. Twelve weekly units, about
        three months, at four to five hours a week. You can pause at any point.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Founders Edition stands on its own. It also sits inside the main
        course, as a bridge after Rome, since nearly all of its sources are Roman.
        If you go on to the full course, the Latin you have done carries over.
      </p>
      <p>
        The week is the same engine. Read the primary texts in translation, with a
        short guide. Translate, by Ascham’s method, a short Latin passage the
        founders knew. Look at a building, a seal, or a painting. Reflect, in about
        three hundred words: what did the founders take, and what did they change?
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow prose">
      <h2>The scholarship underneath</h2>
      <p>Three books are the spine, and the disagreements among them are part of the teaching.</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>
          Carl J. Richard, <em>The Founders and the Classics: Greece, Rome, and the
          American Enlightenment</em> (Harvard University Press, 1994), and his
          <em>Greeks and Romans Bearing Gifts</em> (2008).
        </li>
        <li>
          Bernard Bailyn, <em>The Ideological Origins of the American Revolution</em>
          (Belknap Press of Harvard University Press, 1967). Bailyn argues that
          classical references were often illustrative, and that the English
          Commonwealth radicals, such as Trenchard and Gordon’s <em>Cato’s Letters</em>,
          shaped the Revolution’s thinking more directly. That counterweight is taught
          openly.
        </li>
        <li>
          Gordon S. Wood, <em>The Creation of the American Republic, 1776–1787</em> (1969).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {
    parts.map((part) => (
      <section class="section">
        <div class="wrap-narrow">
          <h2>{part.title}</h2>
          <ol class="weeks">
            {part.units.map((unit) => (
              <li class="week">
                <h3>
                  <span class="kicker" style="display:block;margin-bottom:0.3rem">
                    Unit {unit.n}
                  </span>
                  {unit.title}
                </h3>
                <p>{unit.body}</p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow prose">
      <h2>Latin, and a little Greek</h2>
      <p>
        Latin is the core: a compressed start in Wheelock chapters 1–10, or
        <em>Lingua Latina</em> chapters 1–8. The double-translation texts are Latin
        the founders knew: Cicero in <em>De Re Publica</em> and <em>De Officiis</em>,
        Livy on Cincinnatus, the Virgil behind the Great Seal, and the Seal’s own mottoes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Greek is light and optional. The alphabet, and a political vocabulary:
        <em>dēmokratia</em>, <em>politeia</em>, <em>aristokratia</em>, <em>tyrannos</em>,
        <em>nomos</em>, with short passages from Polybius 6 and Aristotle’s
        <em>Politics</em> beside a translation.
      </p>
      <p>
        This edition is an outline, not an open course. Pricing has not been set.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button" href="/waitlist">Join the waitlist</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
